                <h2>Weekly Watch: Localization Is More Than Just 'Translating to Local Language'</h2>
<p>When entering Southeast Asia and LATAM markets, localization is almost a mandatory skill for every advertising team. However, in practice, many teams simply treat localization as "hiring someone to translate ad copy," ignoring critical factors like cultural adaptation, platform rule differences, and payment scenarios.</p>
<p>After discussing with three overseas teams' advertising leads this week, we've compiled five high-frequency mistakes for your reference.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Mistake 1: Directly Translating English Assets Without Visual Localization</h2>
<p>A utility app in Indonesia ran ads using Western-style English creatives with only titles and descriptions translated to Indonesian. However, their click-through rate was significantly lower than expected.</p>
<p>The issue: Southeast Asian users, especially in lower-tier markets, have lower trust in "English-only" interfaces. Localization isn't just language conversion—it also includes:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Visual style</strong>: Indonesian users prefer vibrant colors and local model faces; Brazilian users are more receptive to animated GIFs and football elements</li>
<li><strong>Usage scenarios</strong>: Show localized use cases (local transportation, local festivals)</li>
<li><strong>Trust signals</strong>: Add local payment logos, service promises in local language</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Recommendation</strong>: Incorporate localization perspective during asset creation, not as an afterthought.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Mistake 2: Treating Southeast Asia as "One Market" Ignoring Country Differences</h2>
<p>Many teams use identical assets across Vietnam, Philippines, Thailand, and Indonesia. In reality, these four countries differ significantly in:</p>
<ul>
<li>Preferred social platforms (Vietnamese users favor Zalo over Facebook)</li>
<li>Payment habits (GCash penetration is high in Philippines, while Indonesia still relies on bank transfers)</li>
<li>Cultural taboos (Buddhist vs. Islamic nations)</li>
</ul>
<p>This week, a cross-border e-commerce team found that their Thailand performance was consistently poor. After investigation, they discovered the creatives contained inappropriate gestures (certain gestures are offensive in Thai culture).</p>
<p><strong>Recommendation</strong>: Split ad groups by country with dedicated assets and strategies.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Mistake 3: Ignoring Spanish Dialect Variations in LATAM</h2>
<p>Spanish varies significantly across LATAM countries: Argentine users use "vos" instead of "tú," Mexican slang differs greatly from other nations. One e-commerce team used identical Spanish assets for Mexico and Colombia, leading Colombian users to comment "the tone feels too abrupt."</p>
<p>More seriously, Colombia has strict regulations on certain categories (especially beauty and health products). Generic phrases like "miraculous effects" or "instant results" easily trigger review flags.</p>
<p><strong>Recommendation</strong>:<br />
1. Adapt dialects for key markets (Mexico, Brazil, Colombia, Argentina)<br />
2. Research each country's advertising policies and identify prohibited terms before launching<br />
3. If budget is limited, prioritize Mexico and Brazil localization (larger volume, higher tolerance)</p>
<hr />
<h2>Mistake 4: Account Recharge and Payment Methods Don't Match Local Habits</h2>
<p>This week, a gaming overseas team encountered this situation: during peak spending periods in Brazil, their ad account ran low on balance, but local bank transfers take 2-3 business days to process, causing campaign interruptions and disrupted pacing plans.</p>
<p>LATAM has another characteristic: credit card penetration is not high. Many users prefer installment payments. If the landing page doesn't accommodate local payment methods (like Mercado Pago, OXXO), the conversion funnel will leak significant users at the payment stage.</p>
<p><strong>Recommendation</strong>:<br />
- Set up account balance alerts and arrange recharges in advance<br />
- Add local mainstream payment methods on landing pages<br />
- Consider partnering with local agents to solve payment and tax issues</p>
<hr />
<h2>Mistake 5: Directly Copying Domestic "Viral Creative" Formulas to Overseas Markets</h2>
<p>Tactics common in Chinese information flow ads—countdown timers, "limited quantity" claims, expert endorsements—may work partially in Southeast Asia and LATAM, but risks exist:</p>
<ul>
<li>Indonesia is tightening regulations on "fake scarcity"; violating creatives get removed</li>
<li>Brazilian consumers have low trust in "exaggerated efficacy" ads and value authentic user reviews more</li>
<li>Some LATAM countries require endorser qualifications; unqualified "experts" may trigger compliance issues</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Recommendation</strong>: Research local competitor creatives before launching to understand real user preferences and avoid red lines.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Weekly Takeaway: Localization Is Systematic Work, Not a Translation Add-on</h2>
<p>Southeast Asia and LATAM offer significant opportunities, but localization barriers are higher than expected. We recommend incorporating localization into your advertising SOP from project inception:</p>
<ul>
<li>✓ Asset localization checklist</li>
<li>✓ Country advertising policy quick reference</li>
<li>✓ Local payment method adaptation</li>
<li>✓ Account recharge and balance alert mechanisms</li>
</ul>
<p>Proper localization converts traffic into users; without it, you're simply burning budget.</p>
